From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Saiem Amer <saiem.amer07@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(terminal): keep the app's own orca first on PATH after
 login startup files

Orca prepends its bundled `Contents/Resources/bin` to the PATH of every
terminal it opens, so agents reach this app's CLI as bare `orca`. Terminals
start as login shells, though, and on macOS `/etc/zprofile` runs path_helper,
which moves the `/etc/paths` entries (starting with `/usr/local/bin`) back in
front. When `/usr/local/bin/orca` belongs to another install (an RC beside
stable, an older copy left behind, a fork), `orca` in an Orca terminal runs
that CLI instead, and `orca orchestration ...` talks to the wrong app or fails
on a command it does not have. orca-cli-child-path.ts already noted the
reorder but nothing undid it.

Name the bundled folder in the PTY env as ORCA_BUNDLED_CLI_BIN_DIR on packaged
macOS, count it as an overlay key so the pane is wrapped, and re-prepend it
from the zsh deferred hook and both bash rcfiles after the user's startup
files have run. The block does nothing unless the folder holds an executable
`orca`, so the relay and SSH copies of the wrapper are inert. Each pane with
the variable is now wrapped, which also gives it the OSC 133 markers and the
zsh history handling that panes with a Codex home already get.

The new test runs real zsh and bash for the local and daemon wrappers, with
a `.zprofile` / `.bash_profile` that puts a stand-in `orca` first; it fails
without either the restore block or the overlay key.
